{"objection": "The argument for jointness rests on a false dichotomy, and the structure it ends up endorsing is several liability, not joint liability. The reductio claims that several obligation would turn one $100 promise into n debts of $100. That holds only if each branch's duty has an agent-relative content: 'that I pay $100.' A promise's content is fixed by what was promised, which is that the promisee receives $100. Suppose each branch is severally bound to see to it that this one outcome obtains. Then there is one debt with n obligors, and one performance satisfies every branch's duty, because the single content is now fulfilled. This is exactly the liability the author borrows from law. 'Each branch is answerable, but performance by one discharges all' is the standard description of joint-and-several liability, and the 'several' half of that phrase is what makes each branch answerable. So the thesis's contrastive claim ('jointly and not severally') contradicts the structure the argument defends. The appeal to the promisee's interest in one performance cannot decide between the views, because both predict single satisfaction. Once the multiplication worry is gone, nothing drives us to a collective obligation-bearer. The distributive reading inherited from Parfitian fission (each successor bound to an outcome-directed duty) is simpler, and it fits Parfit's own suggestion that what matters is inherited by each product. The collective-obligation machinery then cuts against the author. Collins (2013, 'Collectives' Duties and Collectivization Duties'; 2019, Group Duties) denies that unstructured collections can bear duties at all. Such collections get only member-level duties to form a collective or to coordinate, and these presuppose that members can communicate. Discontinuous parallel instances are the paradigm case where they cannot. Björnsson's shared obligations are likewise distributive 'we-ought' claims, grounded in joint ability, that place obligations on each member. Neither theorist supplies an irreducible joint duty for these cases, so the cited theory delivers several obligations. Finally, the distinctive blame verdict is idle. In fission each successor typically inherits full capacity to perform, so 'no branch is blameworthy unless it could have performed' leaves every non-performing branch blameworthy, which is what the several view already predicts.", "severity": 4, "rationale": "This objection removes the paper's main argument, the multiplication reductio, and shows the defended structure to be several liability under another name. The thesis is left with no distinctive content beyond 'one performance discharges the promise,' which rival views share. It is not fatal (5) because a more modest paper could survive. Such a paper might argue that discontinuity creates a coordination problem that the distributive view handles badly: a branch cannot know whether another has paid, so outcome-directed several duties may either overdetermine performance or leave every branch excused. A jointness thesis could be rebuilt around that problem. But that would be a different argument, and it would have to confront Collins's claim that a coordination failure among non-communicating agents produces no group duty at all. As written, the core contrast does not survive."}
